Reviewed `src/blog/people/drafts/David-Beckham.md` and appended a `FRESH EYES REVIEW` block at the bottom. No body edits.

**The two biggest flags:**

1. **His mother is not in the draft.** The whole origin thesis rests on Ted — the father who made him strike balls until dark and withheld praise between milestones — and there's exactly one parent in the piece. Sandra says in the Netflix series that she thought Ted was "too strict," which is the only voice in the public record that judges the machine that made him. And Ted and Sandra divorced in 2002 after 33 years, with Ted's own memoir citing David's fame as a stress on the marriage. That's the darkest possible payoff for the draft's own argument — the delivery engine his father built helped cost his father his marriage, the same year Posh and Becks peaked — and it isn't mentioned. (Sandra was a hairdresser, one section away from an H2 about haircuts as brand strategy.)

2. **The Qatar section dodges the contradiction that's actually his.** It cites Qatar's criminalization of same-sex relationships but never mentions Beckham's twenty years of cultivated gay-icon status — the 2002 *Attitude* cover, "very honoured to have the tag of gay icon" (BBC, 2007), the metrosexual label built largely on him. Without that, the section is generic sportswashing criticism that fits any ambassador; with it, it's specifically about Beckham, and Joe Lycett's stunt stops looking arbitrary.

Also flagged: no Wimbledon halfway-line goal and no rendering of the actual free-kick craft (a piece arguing the gift was manufactured never shows the manufactured thing); Inter Miami referenced three times but never dramatized; "services to sport and charity" in the knighthood with zero charity in the draft; and the beekeeping thread played three times, with the closer restating beats the reader already has.

Sources: [Attitude/metrosexual](https://marksimpson.com/meet-the-metrosexual/), [Beckham's parents](https://www.yahoo.com/entertainment/david-beckhams-parents-ted-beckham-183309719.html)
